      "id": "mechanism.no-incoming",
      "layer": "mechanism",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "section": "8",
      "subsection": null,
      "heading": "No incoming — the supply reading of \"stuck\"",
      "name": "No incoming",
      "nameSource": "heading",
      "fullName": "No incoming — the supply reading of \"stuck\"",
      "fields": [
        {
          "label": "what it is",
          "labelStyle": "label",
          "value": "she's a Generator with strong response energy and, when working alone, **nothing incoming to respond to.** Solo work removes the source. No stimulus, no gut response, no fuel. It reads as being stuck or as a discipline problem, and it's neither — it's a supply problem. `[arguable]` — she said \"it is a supply issue unless it's the same thing as the loop\"",
          "items": [],
          "tags": [
            {
              "raw": "arguable",
              "bases": [
                "arguable"
              ],
              "qualifier": "",
              "pending": false,
              "bookmarked": false,
              "note": "she said \"it is a supply issue unless it's the same thing as the loop\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "her life-force framing",
          "labelStyle": "label",
          "value": "her work is life force; she can't currently locate her own. Read: the signal she's looking for is the gut response, and it only fires on contact with something arriving. No incoming, no response, no signal. It reads as absence of life force. It's absence of stimulus. A supply problem is a better problem than a depletion problem.",
          "items": [],
          "tags": []
        },
        {
          "label": "the counterintuitive fix",
          "labelStyle": "label",
          "value": "stop working on the work; generate incoming. Put unfinished things where people can react. Ask three people a direct question. Publish the half-formed piece. Not to persuade — to create something to respond to. Then respond to that.",
          "items": [],
          "tags": []
        }
      ],
